# PURPOSE
# -------
# Document the core computer science and software engineering 
# concepts applied in the development of the MW–M31 halo movie 
# pipeline. This includes algorithm design, data structures, 
# optimization strategies, and principles of reproducibility.
#
# 1. MODULAR DESIGN
# -----------------
# - The pipeline is divided into discrete modules for:
#       • Inertia tensor and axis ratio computation
#       • Convex hull analysis
#       • Radial binning and velocity anisotropy
#       • Density map projection
#       • Movie generation using FFMpegWriter
# - Benefits:
#       • Easier debugging and testing
#       • Reusable components for other simulations
#       • Separation of concerns (single responsibility principle)
#
# 2. DATA STRUCTURES
# ------------------
# - Numpy arrays:
#       • Efficient storage and vectorized computation of particle positions, 
#         velocities, and masses
#       • Supports broadcasting for mass-weighted operations
#       • Enables O(N) operations instead of nested loops
# - HDF5 files:
#       • Hierarchical data storage for metrics (axis ratios, volumes, triaxiality)
#       • Metadata storage (centering method, snapshot count, units)
#       • Supports reproducibility and versioning of results
# - Matplotlib objects:
#       • Figures and axes stored as objects for iterative frame updates
#       • Enables clean frame-by-frame animation without retaining entire dataset
#
# 3. ALGORITHMIC PRINCIPLES
# --------------------------
# - Vectorization:
#       • Used for inertia tensor, velocity components, radial calculations
#       • Reduces algorithmic complexity from O(N*3^2) to O(N*3)
# - Convex hull computation:
#       • Uses scipy.spatial.ConvexHull with complexity O(M log M)
#       • Only hull vertices are used for axis ratio computation (space optimization)
# - Radial binning:
#       • Logarithmic bins provide balanced resolution across halo scales
#       • Enables O(N) assignment of particles to bins
# - Movie generation:
#       • FFMpegWriter writes frames sequentially
#       • Avoids storing entire animation in memory
#
# 4. TIME AND SPACE COMPLEXITY CONSIDERATIONS
# -------------------------------------------
# - Time complexity:
#       • Inertia tensor: O(N) per snapshot
#       • Convex hull: O(M log M) per snapshot, M << N
#       • Radial velocity and anisotropy: O(N_bins * N_particles)
#       • Density map: O(N_particles)
#       • Overall pipeline scales linearly with number of snapshots
# - Space complexity:
#       • Preallocated arrays: O(n_snapshots) for metrics
#       • Density grids: O(bins^2)
#       • Radial arrays: O(N_bins)
#       • Avoids retaining full particle arrays for all snapshots
#
# 5. PARALLELIZATION AND PERFORMANCE
# ----------------------------------
# - Movies and snapshot metrics can be parallelized:
#       • Snapshot-level parallelism (independent computations)
#       • Radial bin computations can be vectorized per thread
# - Optimized memory usage prevents memory leaks during long loops
# - Performance monitored using print logging per snapshot
#
# 6. SOFTWARE ENGINEERING PRINCIPLES
# ----------------------------------
# - Reproducibility:
#       • HDF5 files store computed metrics, metadata, and configuration
# - Documentation:
#       • Each module contains docstrings explaining purpose, inputs, outputs
# - Logging:
#       • Prints snapshot-level processing info for monitoring
# - Configurability:
#       • User-defined centering method, radial bins, spatial limits, and movie parameters
# - Extensibility:
#       • Easy to add new movie types (stellar component, gas particles)
#
# 7. NUMERICAL METHODS
# --------------------
# - Eigenvalue decomposition for inertia tensors
# - Covariance matrix computation for hull vertex axis ratios
# - Standard deviation for velocity dispersion
# - Logarithmic scaling for radial bins and density visualization
# - All operations vectorized for speed and accuracy
#
# 8. ERROR HANDLING AND ROBUSTNESS
# --------------------------------
# - Conditional checks for radial bins with insufficient particles
# - Safe handling of zero radial dispersion (β=0)
# - Input validation for centering methods
# - Validation against synthetic ellipsoids to ensure metric accuracy
#
# 9. CONCLUSION
# -------------
# The MW–M31 movie pipeline exemplifies careful application of 
# computer science principles in scientific computing. Its design 
# emphasizes modularity, reproducibility, efficient data structures, 
# and computational optimization. These principles enable the 
# creation of high-resolution, publication-ready movies that 
# provide deep physical insights into halo dynamics during a 
# major galaxy merger.
